 3603, 6518, 13811, +more)]\\n\\nThe relationships depict BIOPSY as a bridge between suspicion or clinical findings (e.g., lymph node concerns) and confirmatory laboratory evaluation (“body tissue or fluid testing”), which then informs planned treatment sequencing. In practical terms, BIOPSY sits at the point where diagnostic uncertainty becomes a classified diagnosis and a pathway to treatment planning. [Data: Entities (1097, 2140); Relationships (1234, 1235, 1736, 3603, 3269, +more)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"The community distinguishes tissue-based vs fluid-based diagnostic sampling, both obtainable via biopsy\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Two major specimen modalities are explicit: TISSUE SAMPLE and FLUID/FLUID SAMPLE. A TISSUE SAMPLE is defined as tissue removed from a suspicious or abnormal area during biopsy and then examined to confirm or clarify cancer and characterize disease features. [Data: Entities (509, 84); Relationships (1736)]\\n\\nIn parallel, the network emphasizes that biopsy can also obtain FLUID and a FLUID SAMPLE for diagnostic evaluation (e.g., via aspiration- or biopsy-related procedures). This positions fluid cytology/analysis as a sibling pathway to histologic tissue assessment within the broader biopsy-centered diagnostic workflow. [Data: Entities (1550, 2084, 84); Relationships (2525, 4591, 1736)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Pathology interpretation and biomarker testing are downstream capabilities enabled by biopsy-obtained specimens\",\n      \"explanation\": \"A key capability in this community is laboratory/pathology examination of biopsy-obtained material: TISSUE SAMPLE is described as being sent for microscopic examination to determine whether cancer (or pre-cancer) is present and to classify the disease, and it is analyzed by a PATHOLOGIST to characterize the cancer. [Data: Entities (509); Relationships (1156)]\\n\\nBeyond basic diagnosis, the community highlights that biopsy material supports biomarker and 