ips (13133)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Ovarian-cancer workflow emphasizes biopsy during initial surgery and links biopsy timing to planned treatment\",\n      \"explanation\": \"In ovarian cancer, BIOPSY is typically performed during INITIAL SURGERY, where it both confirms diagnosis and supports cancer removal and staging-related information gathering. This tightly couples diagnostic confirmation to the first major surgical intervention in that care pathway. [Data: Entities (2105, 84); Relationships (3598)]\\n\\nThe network also captures that biopsy timing can vary based on PLANNED TREATMENT needs—biopsy may be done before surgery or other planned treatment when required to diagnose ovarian cancer and guide next steps. This highlights biopsy’s role as a gating step that can occur either within surgery or prior to it, depending on clinical sequencing. [Data: Entities (2140); Relationships (3603)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Specific diagnostic endpoints appear as examples where biopsy confirmation triggers further evaluation (anal SCC; bladder tissue sample)\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community includes an explicit diagnosis node—ANAL SQUAMOUS CELL CARCINOMA—linked to BIOPSY with the statement that after biopsy confirms this diagnosis, further testing is needed to plan treatment and assess spread. This illustrates biopsy’s role as the confirmation step that unlocks subsequent staging/treatment workups. [Data: Entities (6046); Relationships (13811)]\\n\\nSimilarly, BLADDER TISSUE SAMPLE is linked to BIOPSY to represent analysis of surgically removed bladder tissue for bladder cancer assessment. Together with endoscopic viewing/biopsy capability (via cystoscope), this reinforces that biopsy can be embedded in organ-specific diagnostic pathways. [Data: Entities (5754); Relationships (12990, 13133)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"The community reflects repeat/monitoring use-cases and post-procedure practicalities (confirmatory biopsy